    <!-- ═══ S7 – CURRENT / NON-CURRENT ═══ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7">
      <span class="num">7</span><h2>
        Current vs Non-Current Classification
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Current asset – any ONE of four tests</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span
            >Expected to be realised, sold, or consumed in the entity's <strong
              >normal operating cycle</strong
            ></span
          >
          <span>Held primarily <strong>for the purpose of trading</strong></span
          >
          <span
            >Expected to be realised <strong>within twelve months</strong> after the
            reporting period</span
          >
          <span
            >Is <strong>cash or a cash equivalent</strong>, unless restricted
            from exchange/use to settle a liability for at least twelve months</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          All other assets are non-current. The operating cycle test means
          inventory and trade receivables are current even when the cycle
          exceeds twelve months (e.g. long-term construction; ageing wines;
          whisky).
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Current liability – any ONE of four tests</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span
            >Expected to be settled in the entity's <strong
              >normal operating cycle</strong
            ></span
          >
          <span>Held primarily <strong>for the purpose of trading</strong></span
          >
          <span
            >Due to be settled <strong>within twelve months</strong> after the reporting
            period</span
          >
          <span
            >The entity does <strong>NOT have an unconditional right</strong> to defer
            settlement for at least twelve months after the reporting period</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <strong>Covenant breach trap:</strong> if a loan becomes repayable on demand
          due to a covenant breach at the reporting date, it is classified as CURRENT
          – even if the lender subsequently waives the breach after year-end but before
          authorisation of issue. The right to defer must exist AT the reporting date
          itself. This is among the most heavily examined IAS 1 classification points.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══ S8 – SoPL STRUCTURE ═══ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8">
      <span class="num">8</span><h2>
        Statement of Profit or Loss &amp; OCI – Structure
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">
          Presentation choices – two major policy decisions
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">CHOICE 1 – SINGLE STATEMENT OR TWO STATEMENTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list" style="margin-bottom:9px;">
          <span
            ><strong>Single statement:</strong> one statement of profit or loss and
            other comprehensive income; profit/loss appears as a subtotal; total comprehensive
            income is the final total</span
          >
          <span
            ><strong>Two statements:</strong> a separate statement of profit or loss
            (ending with profit/loss for the period); followed immediately by a statement
            of OCI beginning with profit/loss and ending with total comprehensive
            income</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">CHOICE 2 – EXPENSE CLASSIFICATION</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span
            ><strong>By function (cost of sales method):</strong> expenses grouped
            by their function – cost of sales; distribution; administrative; finance;
            other. A cost-of-sales line and gross profit subtotal appear. Entities
            using this method must disclose by-nature information (depreciation; employee
            benefits) in the notes.</span
          >
          <span
            ><strong>By nature (nature of expense method):</strong> expenses grouped
            by their nature – raw materials; employee benefits; depreciation; other.
            No cost-of-sales line. Typically used by smaller or manufacturing-focused
            entities.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          "By function" provides more analysis on the face but requires
          additional by-nature disclosures in the notes – so it demands MORE
          total disclosure overall.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Minimum line items on the face of the SoPL</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Revenue (disaggregated as required by IFRS 15)</span>
          <span>Finance costs</span>
          <span
            >Share of profit/loss of associates and joint ventures (equity
            method – IAS 28)</span
          >
          <span>Tax expense (IAS 12)</span>
          <span
            >A single line for total of discontinued operations (IFRS 5)</span
          >
          <span>Profit or loss for the period</span>
          <span>Each component of OCI classified by recycling group</span>
          <span>Total comprehensive income</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:6px;margin-bottom:0;">
          <strong>Subtotals:</strong> additional subtotals may be presented (e.g.
          gross profit; operating profit; EBIT) when they are relevant to understanding
          the entity's performance. They must be: (a) comprised of items recognised
          and measured under IFRS; (b) presented and labelled in a manner that is
          not misleading; (c) consistent from period to period.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

